#include <algorithm>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>

struct Level {
    long long price;   // pence
    int size;
};

bool operator<(const Level& a, const Level& b) {
    return a.price < b.price;
}

class Book {
public:
    void add(bool is_bid, long long price, int size) {
        if (size <= 0) throw std::invalid_argument("size must be positive");
        if (is_bid && !asks_.empty() && price >= asks_.front().price)
            throw std::invalid_argument("bid would cross the book");
        if (!is_bid && !bids_.empty() && price <= bids_.front().price)
            throw std::invalid_argument("ask would cross the book");
        std::vector<Level>& side = is_bid ? bids_ : asks_;
        for (Level& l : side) {
            if (l.price == price) { l.size += size; return; }
        }
        side.push_back(Level{price, size});
        std::sort(side.begin(), side.end());
        if (is_bid) std::reverse(side.begin(), side.end());
    }
    long long best_bid() const {
        if (bids_.empty()) throw std::logic_error("no bids");
        return bids_.front().price;
    }
    long long best_ask() const {
        if (asks_.empty()) throw std::logic_error("no asks");
        return asks_.front().price;
    }
    long long spread() const { return best_ask() - best_bid(); }
    int depth(bool is_bid) const {
        return static_cast<int>((is_bid ? bids_ : asks_).size());
    }
    int size_at(bool is_bid, long long price) const {
        for (const Level& l : is_bid ? bids_ : asks_)
            if (l.price == price) return l.size;
        return 0;
    }

private:
    std::vector<Level> bids_;   // best (highest) first
    std::vector<Level> asks_;   // best (lowest) first
};

items.model:
**(a) Invariants, and where they are checked.**

1. The ticker is not empty. *Checked in the constructor; nothing changes it afterwards.*
2. $|q| \le$ the position limit, and the limit is positive. *Checked in the constructor and in `buy` and `sell` before anything changes.*
3. A flat position ($q = 0$) has average price 0; otherwise the average is the weighted entry price of the open quantity. *Kept by `buy` and `sell`, the only functions that change $q$.*
4. Realised P&L changes only when a trade reduces the position. *Kept by `sell` (and `buy` covering a short).*

**(b) Declaration.**

```cpp
class Position {
public:
    Position(std::string ticker, long long limit);
    void buy(long long qty, long long px_pence);
    void sell(long long qty, long long px_pence);
    const std::string& ticker() const;
    long long qty() const;
    double avg_price_pence() const;
    long long realised_pence() const;
private:
    std::string ticker_;
    long long limit_;
    long long qty_ = 0;
    double avg_ = 0.0;
    long long realised_ = 0;
};
```

The data is private, so only `buy` and `sell` can change it. Readers are `const`, and `ticker()` returns a `const` reference, never a non-const one, so no caller can write through it. The constructor builds members in its initialiser list, in declaration order.

**(c) Special members.** None: the rule of zero. Every member (`std::string`, integers, a `double`) copies, moves and destroys itself correctly, so the compiler's versions are right. Copies are wanted (reports take snapshots), and moves are cheap. A raw pointer or handle member would force the rule of five; there is none.

**(d) Operators.** `operator<<(std::ostream&, const Position&)` for logs, returning the stream. `operator==` only if a test needs it, comparing every field. I would **not** define `operator+` on two positions. Adding AZN to VOD is meaningless, and even for one ticker it hides which trades happened. Changes go through named functions, `buy` and `sell`. Summed P&L amounts get their own `Money` or `Pnl` type, so a quantity can never be added to money.

**(e) Money and errors.** Prices and P&L are whole pence in `long long`, so sums are exact. Only the average, a ratio, is a `double`, and it is never compared with `==`. A broken rule throws before any member changes, so a refused call leaves the position exactly as it was. Bad input throws `std::invalid_argument`; a breach such as exceeding the limit throws `std::logic_error`.
